    "q-134235": {
      "dom_id": "question-134235",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Of the $3$ pounds of walnuts that Robert bought, he gave $\\frac{3}{5}$ pounds to his granddaughter Sophia. As a mixed number, Robert has\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \npounds of walnuts remaining.",
      "worked_solution": "To find out how many walnuts Robert had remaining, we must calculate the difference $3-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{3}{5}$ from $3,$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $5.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{15}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{15-3}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n12÷5=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Robert had $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ pounds of walnuts remaining.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
